% ==============================================================================
% V. PROPOSED METHOD
% ==============================================================================
\section{Proposed Framework}
\label{sec:proposed}

% PROVISIONAL SCOPE. Every structural claim in this subsection (shared trunk, two
% continuous heads, one categorical head, and which quantity each head emits)
% describes the proposed policy, which is not yet implemented. Re-verify against
% the final implementation. The event-driven loop and the transition tuple in the
% second half of the subsection ARE implemented and were read from the code.
\subsection{Hybrid Policy Architecture}
The policy is a multi-head network over a shared trunk that consumes the 17-dimensional observation of Table~\ref{tab:state}. Two continuous heads produce the pre-activation values for $\Delta$ and $p$, which are squashed and then mapped onto their respective ranges by the geometric and linear mappings of Section~\ref{sec:problem}, and one categorical head produces the subchannel distribution. The construction follows the hybrid actor-critic pattern in which discrete and continuous heads share a critic~\cite{fan2019} over the parameterized-action formalism of~\cite{masson2016}. Keeping the trunk shared is what lets the timing decision be conditioned on the contention state that also drives the subchannel decision, since the value of a long silence depends on whether the report that ends it will survive contention. Because the training loop must ask for a decision only when a particular vehicle's interval closes, it is not expressible as a synchronous environment stepped by a single global clock; the environment advances the traffic and the physical layer, and an outer event-driven loop assembles a transition and requests a grant for each vehicle whose interval has just closed. The transition stored for vehicle $i$ at decision $k$ is $(s_k, a_k, R_k, s_{k+1}, \mathrm{done}, \Delta_k)$, where $a_k$ is the raw pre-decoding output of the policy, since the update rule needs the quantity the network actually emitted, and $\Delta_k$ is the measured elapsed time that the $\gamma^{\Delta}$ discount is applied to.

\subsection{Act/Rest Dual-Model Hot-Swap and Deployment Feasibility}
An RSU that learns online has to serve inference while training, and the two workloads compete for the same accelerator. We therefore run two copies of the model. The Act copy serves grants and writes transitions into the replay buffer; when the buffer is ready, the Rest copy trains on the accumulated transitions in the background; and when that training completes, the two exchange roles, with the outgoing Act copy finishing the decision it is serving before handing over. The mechanism is a common harness rather than a component of any single method, and all nine baselines are trained and evaluated through the identical harness, so it contributes nothing to the differences reported in Section~\ref{sec:evaluation}. Its purpose is to establish that the deployment is feasible on hardware an RSU could plausibly carry. Measured on a single GPU shared between the Act and Rest copies, inference latency averaged 1.232\,ms with a 99th percentile of 2.54\,ms, against a mean of 1.210\,ms and a 99th percentile of 2.98\,ms when the two copies were placed on separate GPUs. The single-GPU configuration is the one we claim, since a roadside cabinet is unlikely to host multiple accelerators, and the latency it delivers is two orders of magnitude below the 0.1\,s scheduling step and below the shortest interval the action space can request.

\subsection{Hyperparameter Optimization}
% PROVISIONAL SCOPE. "the nine baselines and ... the proposed policy" and "all ten
% policies" below presuppose that the proposed policy exists. Only the nine
% baselines currently have Optuna search spaces defined in src/hpo.py. Re-verify
% the search space and the policy count once the proposed method is implemented.
Model hyperparameters are tuned per method with Optuna, over search spaces defined separately for each of the nine baselines and for the proposed policy, since a learning rate or an entropy target that suits one learner does not transfer to another. Each trial trains the model and scores it on a composite objective evaluated across multiple seeds in the same environment, and the selected configuration for every method is exported so that the comparison can be reproduced. The reward weights are deliberately excluded from this search. They are held at the fixed values given in Section~\ref{sec:problem} for every trial of every method, so that hyperparameter tuning selects how well a method optimizes the benchmark objective rather than which objective it optimizes. This is a stricter protocol than the one adopted in comparable multi-objective work, which has argued against hand-fixed scalarization weights~\cite{xu20232}, and we accept the resulting loss of per-method reward shaping as the price of a comparison in which all ten policies are scored by one identical rule.

\subsection{Evaluation Methodology}
The environment is built by coupling a microscopic traffic simulator to a physical-layer model rather than by replaying a synthetic mobility trace, and this choice is methodological hygiene rather than a contribution. The argument for bidirectional coupling between road traffic and network simulation is long established, since delivery outcomes change driver behaviour, which changes topology, which in turn changes delivery~\cite{sommer2011}. SUMO supplies the mobility, the car-following and lane-changing models, and the signal programs from which the maximum red duration bounding $\Delta$ is extracted~\cite{lopez2018}, and coupling SUMO to a physical-layer or network model with ETSI-conformant message layers is the accepted validation route for V2X services~\cite{raviglione2024}. Two consequences matter for the results that follow. The traffic-signal features the policy relies on are produced by the same signal programs that hold the vehicles stationary, so the predictability the scheduler exploits is a property of the simulated traffic rather than an artefact injected into the observation, and the contention the congestion term measures arises from the granted transmissions themselves rather than from an assumed load.

% ==============================================================================
% VI. PERFORMANCE EVALUATION
% ==============================================================================
\section{Performance Evaluation}
\label{sec:evaluation}

\subsection{Simulation Setup}

\subsubsection{Traffic and Channel Configuration}
Traffic is generated on an urban grid carrying twenty-five signalized intersections in a five-by-five arrangement, with boundary nodes acting as sources and sinks, two lanes per direction, and per-edge speed limits drawn around a 40\,km/h mean so that the network file reports 32 to 48\,km/h across its edges. Adjacent signalized intersections are 900\,m apart, which is three times the RSU range, so the coverage disc measured in an episode never touches that of a neighbouring intersection and the contention observed at the RSU is produced entirely by the vehicles it schedules. The measured cell is centred on one signalized junction selected at the start of each episode. Each episode advances SUMO at a 0.1\,s step, and measurement begins only after a warm-up of 1200 steps, because the in-range vehicle population is still climbing well beyond the first few hundred steps and a shorter warm-up would make the density axis of the results describe how much of a filling transient each episode happened to contain rather than the density it is labelled with. Table~\ref{tab:sim_params} summarizes the parameters, all of which are read from the implementation rather than restated independently.

