\subsubsection{Baseline Methods}
The proposed framework is compared against nine baselines, listed in Table~\ref{tab:baselines}, all of which are implemented in this environment and driven through the same hybrid action interface, the same replay buffer, and the same Act/Rest harness. Three are foundational reinforcement learning algorithms included as reference points rather than selected by literature search: proximal policy optimization~\cite{schulman2017}, soft actor-critic~\cite{haarnoja2018}, and twin delayed deterministic policy gradient~\cite{fujimoto2018}. Each is used through a wrapper that exposes a three-dimensional continuous action, with the third dimension binned into the four subchannels, and the same wrapper is used for all three so that they remain comparable to one another. For the deterministic method this binning is a known disadvantage, since exploration of the subchannel dimension has to come from action noise large enough to cross a bin boundary, and we report it as the honest reason a purely continuous learner is expected to handle the discrete decision poorly.

Adapting a published method to a different environment necessarily changes it, and we state for each method what was carried over and what was not, since a comparison that hides the subtraction is not a comparison of the published method. The residual multi-agent parameterized-action learner of~\cite{li2026} is ported as its learner only: the original paper handles the V2I side by user grouping followed by convex optimization and learns the V2V side alone, and our uplink is orthogonally subchannelized with no successive interference cancellation, so the non-orthogonal multiple access stage is dropped and what remains is a parameterized-action deterministic policy gradient. The branch-split learner of~\cite{hong2026} transfers with the least modification, since its action is our action without the interval, and its continuous branch is widened from one output to two. The hybrid-action multi-agent proximal policy optimization of~\cite{chen2026} is ported without its semantic half, because we transmit mobility state rather than semantic features, so the importance evaluation module and the continuous compression-ratio action have no referent here and roughly half of the original contribution is not reproduced. The dueling double deep Q-network of~\cite{bai2024} already sits at the infrastructure side and keeps its observation, but our two continuous axes are quantized onto a fixed grid for it, and its prioritized replay is weakened to an in-batch importance reweighting because the replay buffer belongs to the pipeline and samples uniformly for every method. The channel allocation learner of~\cite{cohen2025} keeps its subchannel head, which is the paper's original action, and its mellowmax backup, but its low-dimensional per-transmitter interference observation is replaced by our RSU-side vector with the channel busy ratio and the in-range count playing that role, and since the method contributes no freshness term of its own, the objective it optimizes here is our reward rather than its own global SINR criterion. For~\cite{parvini2023}, both of the paper's contributions are inactive in our pipeline and this is the weakest of the nine ports: the task-decomposed value heads require per-term rewards in the batch and the global critic requires the observations of other agents, and the environment streams neither, so the four heads reduce to copies of one another and the global critic degenerates into a second local critic. What is compared under that name is therefore a reduced variant with both of Parvini \emph{et~al.}'s mechanisms disabled, not the published method.

