\subsection{Training Configuration}
% PROVISIONAL SCOPE. "Every method" is written to include the proposed policy,
% which is not yet implemented. Confirm that the same training budget and the same
% density rotation apply to it unchanged once it exists.
Every method is trained for 100 episodes of 2000 steps in the same environment, under the fixed reward weights of Section~\ref{sec:problem} and with the per-method hyperparameters selected by the search of Section~\ref{sec:proposed}. The density grid is visited in rotation across episodes so that no method is trained on a narrower range of contention than another. Training runs are monitored for divergence by a shared rule applied identically to all methods, so that a run aborted for divergence is aborted by the same criterion regardless of which learner produced it.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}
Policies are scored across the density grid and five evaluation seeds on five quantities. The mean estimation error is the time-averaged residual of the RSU's dead reckoning and is the primary measure of how good the awareness table actually is. Mean and peak AoI are reported alongside it, the latter as the maximum over all vehicles and all times, so that a policy which buys a low average by abandoning a few vehicles is visible. The uplink success rate measures how many granted reports were decoded, mean transmit power measures what the schedule cost in energy, and the channel busy ratio measures what it cost in occupancy. Reporting the error and the age together is deliberate, since the entire argument of this paper is that the two can diverge, and a result in which they moved identically would indicate that the scenario never produced a predictable vehicle.

