% qualitatively because the numeric value in circulation has not been verified
% against the source standard.
The congestion term of~\eqref{eq:reward} is measured as the true airtime occupancy of the RSU's subchannels rather than through a proxy, and that measurement makes the operating regime of this study explicit. A 300-byte cooperative awareness message at the 6\,Mbps base rate holds its subchannel for 448\,\textmu s, which is $0.448\,\%$ of a 100\,ms scheduling step, and two co-channel frames collide only when one begins within one frame duration of the other, so the vulnerable-period overlap probability between any pair of them is $2T_{\mathrm{air}}/T_{\mathrm{step}} = 0.896\,\%$. The cell studied here therefore operates in a lightly loaded regime, far below the levels at which decentralized congestion control mechanisms engage, and the packet losses it produces are governed principally by distance and transmit power rather than by collisions. Two consequences follow, and we state them rather than retuning the scenario to avoid them. The first is that the update-interval trade-off optimized in this paper is driven mainly by estimation error against transmit power and redundancy, with channel occupancy acting as a secondary term; retaining that term is nonetheless deliberate, because its purpose is to establish whether the objective responds correctly to vehicle density rather than to dominate the objective, and a scheduler that omitted it could not say what its silences cost the channel. The second is that regimes in which congestion does dominate, namely denser cells, cooperative perception payloads an order of magnitude larger than a status report, or a shared band carrying background ITS traffic, lie outside the configuration evaluated here and are left to future work.

% ==============================================================================
% VII. CONCLUSION
% ==============================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

% PROVISIONAL SCOPE. This paragraph states what the proposed framework does. The
% proposed policy is not yet implemented, so the description must be re-checked
% against the final design before submission.
This paper proposed a context-aware V2I uplink scheduling framework in which an RSU converts the interval over which a vehicle's state is expected to remain reproducible by dead reckoning into a silence interval granted in advance, and learns that conversion together with the subchannel and the transmit power as a single policy. The scheduling problem was formulated as an SMDP whose decision epochs are the report arrivals of individual vehicles, which is what a decision-dependent interval requires, and the policy emits a hybrid action combining the continuous interval and power with a discrete subchannel index. The reward was constructed so that the estimation error accumulates across the silence interval while power, occupancy, and redundancy are charged once per granted report, which makes a long silence inexpensive for a vehicle whose state is reproducible and expensive for one whose state is not. The observation was restricted to quantities the RSU can obtain without querying any vehicle, so that the scheduler does not consume the uplink capacity it is meant to conserve, and the deployment was measured on a single GPU to confirm that inference latency remains far below the shortest interval the action space can request. Quantitative comparison against the nine implemented baselines is reported in Section~\ref{sec:evaluation} once the training runs currently in progress have completed, and the limitations of the channel-load regime studied here, together with the extension to denser cells and larger cooperative-perception payloads, are left to that discussion and to future work.

